# `exploratory/` — Scratch notebooks (disposable)

Two very different jobs get called "notebooks"; this directory holds only the
**first** kind:

- **Scratch / exploration (HERE).** Looking at data, trying an idea. Disposable,
  read by no one later, held to no lint or reproducibility standard. Nothing here
  is load-bearing.
- **Narrative / tutorial (NOT here → `docs/`).** A curated explanation meant to be
  *read and kept true* — the tutorial that walks through the library/pipeline. That
  lives in `docs/` and is **executed on every docs build**, so it can't silently
  drift from the code. See `docs/tutorial.{% if docs_backend == "sphinx" %}md{% else %}ipynb{% endif %}`.

The deciding question: **is this notebook disposable, or is it kept and read?**
Disposable → here. Kept-and-read → `docs/`. Anything that starts producing a real
figure/table/number **moves into `src/`** (a tested function) called by a
`workflow/` rule — never regenerated from a notebook.

## Tool: {{ notebook_tool }}
{% if notebook_tool == "marimo" -%}
This project uses **marimo**: notebooks are **pure `.py`** — git-diffable, no
stored outputs, reactive (no stale hidden state), runnable as scripts. Far more
reproducible than JSON notebooks (~36% of Jupyter notebooks don't re-run). Edit:
`marimo edit exploratory/example_marimo.py`.
{% elif notebook_tool == "jupyter" -%}
This project uses **Jupyter**. The `nbstripout` pre-commit hook strips outputs
before every commit (clean diffs; no data/credentials leak into git) — **keep it
installed.** Launch: `jupyter lab`. Consider marimo for new projects: pure-`.py`
notebooks avoid the out-of-order / hidden-state problems that make `.ipynb`
unreproducible.
{% else -%}
No exploration tool configured. Re-run `copier update` with a different
`notebook_tool`, or install marimo / Jupyter yourself.
{% endif %}
Everything in `exploratory/` is excluded from ruff/black — exploration is not held
to library lint standards.
